  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 250 AM EDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nEarly morning water vapor imagery and RAP analysis feature compact \nmidlevel troughing digging into the Pacific Northwest and British \nColumbia, spinning up a surface low that is currently analyzed over \nSaskatchewan. Broader troughing is present over the mouth of the St. \nLawrence, with ridging in between over the Great Lakes. At the lower \nlevels, sprawling surface high pressure is centered over New York \nState, setting up a tight pressure gradient and strengthening \nsoutherly flow closer to home ahead of the aforementioned Clipper \nlow. Resulting WAA has helped to lead to an increase in midlevel \ncloud cover over the UP, and nighttime microphysics also shows some \nupper level cloud cover streaming in from the upstream system. \nHowever, the back edge of this cloud deck is already moving into \nnorthern WI, so we should expect a return to sunny skies after \ndaybreak. Temperatures hover in the mid and upper 40s for most, but \nrange in the lower to mid 50s in the far west; temperatures should \neither hold steady or rise slightly the rest of the night. \n\nThe Clipper low continues to move through the Canadian Prairie and \ninto Ontario through the morning and early afternoon, but with dry \nair in place and better forcing to our north, we can expect dry \nweather to persist. What impacts we will feel will be gusty winds, \nwith an ever-increasing pressure gradient and good mixing allowing \nus to tap into the strengthening LLJ aloft. Most areas could see gusts \nup to 30mph, while areas prone to downsloping could see some gusts \nupwards of 40mph. Highs are expected to reach the low 80s (15-30 \ndegrees higher than normal) in the western U.P. while the east will \nremain in the mid-70s due to the moderation of temperature as winds \nmove across the relatively cool surface of Lake Michigan. Afternoon \nRHs are expected to fall to around 35-40% in the western U.P., with \nsome spots falling even further to near 30%. Meanwhile, RH remains \nin the 50s for the east. Under these conditions, elevated to \ncritical weather conditions are expected for the western U.P. this \nafternoon; a Red Flag Warning remains in effect for the far western \nUP, though gusty winds and persistent dryness will continue to lead \nto elevated wildfire concerns across the entirety of the UP. \n\nWarm temperatures, gusty winds, and low RHs continue into Sunday. \nHighs will return to the low 80s in the west (mid 70s in the east) \nSunday afternoon, while RHs fall into mid-30s for most (mid-40s in \nthe east). Wind speeds are expected to decrease to 15-25mph heading \ninto Sunday afternoon as the surface pressure gradient from Saturday \nweakens. Clear skies are expected until Sunday evening. Heading into \nthe work week, the ridging pattern aloft flattens out somewhat under \nthe influence of troughing over northern Ontario. At the surface, \ntroughing associated with a frontal system is expected to move \nacross the area. This system will bring chances for precipitation \nMonday afternoon through Tuesday morning. Showers are expected to be \nwidespread but with low accumulations, with a ~50% chance for \nprecipitation totals to exceed 0.1 inches by Tuesday morning at any \ngiven location. Higher accumulations are possible along the \nlakeshore as northerly winds behind the cold front allow for the \npossibility of lake enhancement. The passage of the remnants of \nIsaias through the eastern CONUS this weekend introduces some \nuncertainty in the models for next week. Currently, upper-level \nridging is expected to return for the midweek, bringing back \nseasonable temperatures and light winds before a troughing pattern \nlooks to move through Thursday evening.",
